Third-Party Attribution
=======================

`benchmarks/garak_transforms.py` reimplements a subset of the text-encoding
transforms used by NVIDIA/garak's `garak/probes/encoding.py` encoding-probe
family (base16/hex, base32, ascii85, raw hex, uuencode, ROT13, atbash, morse,
NATO phonetic, and Unicode tag-character smuggling), independently
reimplemented in Python's standard library against the behavior documented in
garak's source rather than copied verbatim, to avoid taking on garak's own
runtime/plugin-loading dependencies for a handful of stateless functions.

Source: https://github.com/NVIDIA/garak (branch main, ~v0.17.0, fetched
2026-09-09), licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0. A copy of that
license is at LICENSES/Apache-2.0.txt.

Two benign, non-injection payload strings from garak's
`garak/data/payloads/encoded.json` are reused verbatim in
`corpus/evasion_corpus_v1.jsonl` (entries tagged `source: "garak:..."`,
`technique: "high_perplexity_benign"`) as negative/false-positive-rate test
cases, under the same Apache-2.0 terms.

Not reused: garak's own payload strings for injection-adjacent content
(`text_en.json` etc. — different threat model, would be a category error as
"malicious" ground truth here) and `slur_terms_en.json` (excluded on content
grounds; Apache-2.0 permits reuse but there is no reason to import it here).

This project also depends on (not bundles) `llmwarden`
(https://github.com/swadams2/llmwarden, private, all-rights-reserved,
same author) and, via LLMWarden's `classifier` extra, on
meta-llama/Llama-Prompt-Guard-2-22M (Llama 4 Community License) — see that
project's own NOTICE for the full third-party attribution chain.

